# Hand-written NEON forward-XYB kernel — the measurement behind shipping it
#
# host      : Apple silicon, macOS 25.5.0 (aarch64), single-threaded
# repo      : jxl-encoder, workspace ~/work/zen/jxl-encoder--highbitdepth
# base      : c7de490df405 ("zen XYB cube root is cbrt_lowp")
# date      : 2026-09-10
#
# WHAT SHIPPED
#   `forward_xyb_impl`'s `#[magetypes(...)]` list gained `-neon` (and `-scalar`,
#   for a separate reason recorded below), and `forward_xyb_impl_neon` is now
#   hand-written in jxl-encoder-simd/src/xyb.rs. The `incant!` call site is
#   unchanged — the `<fn>_neon` naming convention is what makes a hand-written
#   tier a drop-in.
#
# THE HEADLINE, STATED HONESTLY
#   On the path zen actually ships (`XybCubeRoot::LowP`) the hand-written kernel
#   is a WASH: median 0.989 over 10 runs, spread 0.962-1.014. It is a real win
#   only on `MidP` (median 0.936) and a wash on `Libjxl` (0.986). So this landed
#   because it was asked for and because it is non-regressing everywhere and
#   bit-identical — NOT because it made the encoder faster.
#
#   Data: xyb_neon_handwritten_2026-09-10.tsv (30 rows = 10 runs x 3 cube roots).
#
# METHOD, AND WHY THE FIRST METHOD WAS NOT GOOD ENOUGH
#   Comparing two BINARIES (build A, build B, alternate process invocations,
#   min of N) could not resolve this effect. Four arms in the harness are
#   untouched by the change and serve as controls; across runs they drifted
#   0.957-1.025, i.e. a +-4 % noise floor, while the effect is 1-6 %. Two runs
#   of the same comparison reported the shipped LowP path at 0.991 and at 0.914.
#   Neither number was usable.
#
#   The measurement that IS usable compiles BOTH bodies into ONE binary and
#   calls them back to back inside a single process, alternating which goes
#   first on each repeat. Same page cache, same thermal state, same allocator.
#   That is where the numbers in the .tsv come from.
#
#   Reproducing it needs a temporary scaffold, because the two bodies cannot
#   normally coexist (they claim the same symbol). In jxl-encoder-simd/src/xyb.rs:
#     1. `-neon` -> `neon` in `forward_xyb_impl`'s `#[magetypes(...)]` list, so
#        the generated tier exists again as `forward_xyb_impl_neon`;
#     2. rename the hand-written fn to `forward_xyb_impl_hand_neon`;
#     3. add two `#[doc(hidden)] pub fn` wrappers that `NeonToken::summon()` and
#        call each directly, re-export them from lib.rs;
#     4. an example that times both, alternating order, and diffs the outputs
#        bitwise.
#   The scaffold was deliberately NOT committed: keeping it would mean carrying
#   a second copy of the shared kernel body forever to benchmark against.
#
# TWO CANDIDATES MEASURED AND REJECTED
#   1. NATIVE 128-BIT WIDTH. The first hand-written version worked in f32x4 —
#      NEON's natural width — on the theory that the generated tier's f32x8 is a
#      2x f32x4 polyfill and therefore buys nothing. That theory is WRONG: the
#      128-bit body ran 1.24-1.39x SLOWER (an effect far outside the +-4 % floor
#      of even the weak method). `cbrt_lowp`/`cbrt_midp` build their Kahan guess
#      through `to_array()`/`from_array()`, and LLVM SLP-vectorises that window
#      better at 8 elements than at 4. Width is not free to change.
#   2. VECTORISING THE KAHAN BIT-HACK. `cbrt_lowp` computes its initial guess as
#      `f32::from_bits((bits / 3) + MAGIC)` through a scalar round trip, which
#      looks like the obvious thing to remove. Doing the `/ 3` in integer vector
#      lanes (Hacker's Delight `divu3`, verified EXACT against `n / 3` over the
#      whole u32 range — exactness is mandatory, the result is a bit pattern)
#      costs 0.36 ms/Mvalue against `cbrt_lowp`'s 0.29. LLVM's version of the
#      scalar path is cheaper than 14 vector ops. Kept as
#      `bench_cbrt::cbrt_lowp_vecguess_batch` so the null stays checkable:
#        cargo run --release -p jxl-encoder-simd --example cbrt_candidates
#
# WHY THERE IS NO HEADROOM LEFT HERE
#   The whole transform at 1 Mpx costs 1.19 ms on LowP, of which 0.40 ms is the
#   matrix+bias with the cube root skipped entirely. The remaining ~0.8 ms is
#   3 Mvalues of `cbrt_lowp`, and `cbrt_lowp` standalone measures 0.29 ms/Mvalue
#   = 0.87 ms for 3 M. The kernel is already AT the standalone cube-root
#   throughput: there is no XYB-specific overhead left to remove, and the
#   cube root itself is the fastest of the four candidates measured on
#   2026-09-10 (cbrt_candidates_2026-09-10.*). Further work on this kernel needs
#   a cheaper cube root, not a better loop.
#
# A PRE-EXISTING BUG THE WORK SURFACED — `_scalar` TIER WAS NOT BIT-IDENTICAL
#   The new test `forward_xyb_dispatch_is_bit_identical_to_scalar` compares the
#   dispatched kernel against `forward_xyb_scalar` BITWISE (the pre-existing
#   parity test allows 16 ULP and so could not see this) across all 25 archmage
#   token permutations. It failed on exactly one: everything disabled, i.e. the
#   `_scalar` tier — for ALL THREE cube roots and every n >= 8.
#
#   Cause: magetypes' scalar backend implements `mul_add` as `a * b + c`
#   (simd/impls/scalar.rs), NOT fused, while `forward_xyb_scalar` and every
#   vector backend use a genuinely fused multiply-add. The opsin matrix multiply
#   alone was enough to diverge; the cube root was never the issue.
#
#   Consequence: on a host that cannot summon a vector token — pre-AVX2 x86_64,
#   i686, any architecture magetypes has no backend for — the encoder emitted
#   DIFFERENT BYTES than on one that can. hash_lock_expected.txt is a single
#   committed sidecar, so it could only ever have been correct on one of them.
#   CI never caught it: every CI host summons AVX2 or NEON, and the one job that
#   does reach the scalar tier — Cross (i686-unknown-linux-gnu) — runs `--lib`
#   tests ONLY (ci.yml "Test lib (cross/QEMU)"), while the hash locks live in
#   `tests/it`. The new bitwise test IS a `--lib` test in jxl-encoder-simd, so
#   from this commit on that job is the gate that would have caught it — and it
#   PASSES there with the fix (CI run 34502381016), which is direct confirmation
#   on the one target where the scalar tier is what actually dispatches.
#
#   Verified PRE-EXISTING, not introduced here: the same test dropped into the
#   parent commit's xyb.rs fails identically (14 sizes x 3 cube roots).
#
#   Fixed the same way: `-scalar` in the tier list, and `forward_xyb_impl_scalar`
#   hand-written as a delegation to `forward_xyb_scalar`. There is now exactly
#   one scalar implementation instead of two that happened to be close. No
#   shipping bytes move — no measured platform ever reached that tier.
#
#   A SECOND divergent tier, found by the same test on CI and NOT fixable:
#   `wasm128`. WASM SIMD has no FMA instruction at all — magetypes' wasm128
#   backend implements `mul_add` as multiply-then-add and says so in its own
#   source. Recorded as xyb-001 in docs/SIMD_PARITY_KNOWN_DIVERGENCES.md; the
#   test is `#[ignore]`d on wasm32 only and still gates x86_64 and aarch64.
#
#   THE AUDIT WAS THEN DONE and it is four of five kernels, not one. Method:
#   drop each kernel's existing parity tolerance to 0 ULP / 0.0 abs and see
#   which of the 25 archmage token permutations fail. Every failure was the
#   all-disabled one, i.e. the `_scalar` tier:
#       forward_xyb_impl          diverges  -> FIXED (-scalar + delegate)
#       inverse_xyb_planar_impl   4 ULP     -> FIXED (-scalar + delegate)
#       gaborish_5x5_impl         49 ULP    -> FIXED (-scalar + hand-written;
#                                              delegating would have been WRONG)
#       entropy_coeffs_impl       1/18 cases-> OPEN, entropy-001 in
#                                              docs/SIMD_PARITY_KNOWN_DIVERGENCES.md
#       pixel_domain_loss_impl    clean (uses no mul_add)
#   The general fix is upstream: magetypes' scalar backend is what implements
#   `mul_add` as `a * b + c`, and `f32::mul_add` / `libm::fmaf` are fused. One
#   change there fixes every kernel here at once. magetypes is a sibling repo
#   and is not touched from this one — it is the user's call.
#
# VALIDATION AT THE COMMIT
#   hash locks                     63/63 byte-identical
#   Libjxl strategy byte lock      5/5 byte-identical
#   jxl-encoder-simd               189/189
#   jxl-encoder `it`               527/527 (CJXL_PATH/DJXL_PATH set to
#                                  .ci-libjxl/tools; without them 17 tests
#                                  panic in the version guard, which is the
#                                  guard working, not a failure)
#   clippy -p jxl-encoder-simd --all-targets -D warnings   clean
